WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:04.070
If you’ve been thinking of buying a new
MacBook, and I mean any MacBook, from the

00:00:04.070 --> 00:00:08.800
12” Retina, to the 13” Air, or either
of the Macbook Pro models, you should probably

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:10.410
stop and wait just a couple more months.

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:14.490
There are a few reasons why I think we should
be expecting some new MacBooks very soon.

00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:15.490
Here’s why.

00:00:15.490 --> 00:00:20.890
At Apple’s October Event in 2016, Apple
released their brand new 2016 MacBook Pro,

00:00:20.890 --> 00:00:25.980
and at their 2018 October Event, they revealed
the new MacBook Air, the new Mac Mini and

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:27.810
the 2018 iPad Pro.

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:30.449
And there’s something that both of these
events had in common.

00:00:30.449 --> 00:00:34.760
Many of Apple’s products contain encryption
tools, and in order to sell these products

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:39.909
in certain Eurasian countries, Apple is required
to file each product number with the Eurasian

00:00:39.909 --> 00:00:43.420
Economic Commision before they can legally
sell any of them.

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:48.979
So in 2016, just three days before Apple revealed
the new MacBook Pro, Apple filed three new

00:00:48.979 --> 00:00:54.300
model numbers with the EEC for portable personal
computers, which basically means MacBooks.

00:00:54.300 --> 00:01:00.489
And in 2018, Apple filed model numbers with
the EEC twice, once in July and once just

00:01:00.489 --> 00:01:05.390
6 days before their October Event, and sure
enough, we saw new Macs and the iPad Pro.

00:01:05.390 --> 00:01:10.479
And now in 2019, just two weeks ago, Apple
has registered 6 new model numbers with the

00:01:10.479 --> 00:01:13.840
EEC, described once again, as portable personal
computers.

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:17.740
And when this happens, it usually means we
should be expecting those products to be released

00:01:17.740 --> 00:01:22.159
within a few months, so for this reason, you
probably shouldn’t buy a MacBook right now.

00:01:22.159 --> 00:01:26.211
It’s been two years since Apple refreshed
their 12” Retina MacBook, and it’s by

00:01:26.211 --> 00:01:29.340
far the most underwhelming MacBook out of
their lineup.

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:34.829
The 2018 MacBook Air is newer, costs $100
less, and offers so much more value, so we

00:01:34.829 --> 00:01:37.250
wouldn’t recommend anyone to buy the 12”
MacBook.

00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:41.909
For these reasons, we expect Apple to refresh
the 12” MacBook, either with a spec bump,

00:01:41.909 --> 00:01:43.240
or maybe even a redesign.

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:47.720
There’s also a slight chance that we could
see it move from an intel processor to a custom

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:52.299
ARM mobile chip, similar to Apple’s A-line
of iPhone and iPad chips.

00:01:52.299 --> 00:01:58.689
Back in May, Apple hired Mike Filippo, the
lead CPU and System Architect at ARM for 10

00:01:58.689 --> 00:02:01.819
years, who also worked at both AMD and Intel.

00:02:01.819 --> 00:02:05.409
So this shows that Apple is going full force
towards cutting Intel and using their own

00:02:05.409 --> 00:02:06.689
chips on the Mac.

00:02:06.689 --> 00:02:09.700
However, we most likely won’t see this happen
until next year.

00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:14.320
Until then, Intel has recently released some
awesome new low-end laptop processors, so

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:17.880
there’s a good chance we’ll see a spec
bump on the MacBook Air coming this year.

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:19.470
Now here’s where it gets interesting.

00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:25.050
Apple hasn’t updated the base $1300 MacBook
Pro in over two years, while just a few weeks

00:02:25.050 --> 00:02:28.660
ago, they’ve updated the higher-end touch
Bar models for 2019.

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:33.330
So this makes us think, is Apple really going
to keep the low-end 2017 models for another

00:02:33.330 --> 00:02:37.050
year, or do they have something special planned
for this year’s October event?

00:02:37.050 --> 00:02:40.080
We’re really not sure, but it’d be awesome
if they did.

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:43.050
Last year, Apple did something that nobody
was expecting.

00:02:43.050 --> 00:02:46.590
Just three and a half months after they updated
the MacBook Pros for 2018, they announced

00:02:46.590 --> 00:02:49.790
new Vega graphics options for the 15” MacBook
Pro.

00:02:49.790 --> 00:02:55.510
And this year’s 2019 MacBook Pro refresh
can now come with a very powerful 8-core processor,

00:02:55.510 --> 00:03:00.400
and after a lot of testing, we realized that
the bottleneck is now the Vega 20 graphics,

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:04.370
which hasn’t been updated since last year,
so what if Apple is planning to bring a new

00:03:04.370 --> 00:03:09.530
graphics option to the 15” MacBook Pro at
their October Event, just like last year?

00:03:09.530 --> 00:03:15.360
Well, developer Steve Moser discovered a list
of eight unreleased AMD Radeon GPUs that he

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:20.330
found in the second beta of MacOS Catalina,
which will be going live in September.

00:03:20.330 --> 00:03:24.720
Most of them are labelled RX Vega 20 graphics,
but those could be intentionally misnamed

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:26.980
so as to not give away the actual graphics
name.

00:03:26.980 --> 00:03:30.910
There’s a chance these could be tied to
the new Mac Pro, the 15” MacBook Pro or

00:03:30.910 --> 00:03:35.720
maybe even the rumored 16” MacBook Pro that
everyone’s been talking about recently.

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:42.590
AMD has recently launched their RX 5700 series
Navi 7nanometer GPUs, so there’s a chance

00:03:42.590 --> 00:03:47.950
one or more of these recently discovered GPUs
could be a 5700 series chip, and if so, Apple

00:03:47.950 --> 00:03:51.930
could easily update the 15” MacBook Pro
with the new chips later this year.

00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:56.530
And for the 16” MacBook Pro, this GPU discovery
hints at a chance that it’ll actually be

00:03:56.530 --> 00:04:00.230
announced this year, but it might not happen,
so we shouldn’t get our hopes up.

00:04:00.230 --> 00:04:03.810
So for all of those reasons, we definitely
don’t recommend buying a new MacBook right

00:04:03.810 --> 00:04:08.890
now, especially if you’re looking at a lower-end
12” MacBook or 13” MacBook Air, which

00:04:08.890 --> 00:04:10.820
are most likely to be updated.

00:04:10.820 --> 00:04:14.650
So if you can wait another 4 months until
Apple’s October Event, I would definitely

00:04:14.650 --> 00:04:15.860
recommend doing so.

00:04:15.860 --> 00:04:19.060
If you enjoyed this video, make sure to tap
that like button, click that circle above

00:04:19.060 --> 00:04:21.650
to subscribe, and check out one of those two
videos right there.

00:04:21.650 --> 00:04:26.790
Thanks for watching, and we’ll see you in
the next one!

